HISTORY_VERSION 1.0.0

TYPE Foundation

STATUS APPROVED

DATE 2026-07-06

TITLE

Validation factored into Services; Kernel now loads the Instance Contract from the real file instead of a hand transcription.

WHY

Reviewing the Kernel implementation surfaced two honest gaps: validation
logic (required-field checks, required-folder checks) lived inline
inside kernel.py rather than in services/, contradicting the project's
own layering (Services = shared infrastructure Kernel and future engines
depend on). And "loading the contract" was, in truth, a set of Python
constants transcribed by hand from contracts/INSTANCE_CONTRACT_0.1.0.pdl
— the Kernel never actually read that file at runtime, so the two could
silently drift apart. Both were named explicitly rather than let ride.

DECISION

Added services/validate.py: require_fields, require_dirs,
require_non_empty_dir, forbid_file_suffixes — small, reusable checks, not
owned by Kernel alone.

Added services/pdl_nested.py: nested-section extraction on top of the
flat PDL grammar, documented in the new protocols/PDL_SPECIFICATION_0.2.0.pdl
(supersedes nothing — adds to 0.1.0). Deliberately does not attempt a
fully generic indentation-based parser: these documents mix structural
indentation with hand-wrapped prose indentation, which cannot be told
apart without guessing. Instead, the caller supplies a known, fixed
vocabulary of top-level section names — the same technique the old
system's own BOOTSTRAP_CONTEXT.pdl already used via its READ_ORDER list.

Added services/contract_loader.py, built on pdl_nested.py, that actually
parses contracts/INSTANCE_CONTRACT_0.1.0.pdl at runtime and extracts the
real ROOT_MANIFEST required fields and the real SECTIONS list. Verified
directly against the live file (not a synthetic example) via
services/tests/test_contract_loader.py — all 4 assertions passing,
matching the file's actual content exactly.

Rewrote kernel/kernel.py to call these services instead of holding its
own copies. SUPPORTED_SCHEMA_VERSIONS and the forbidden-suffix list stay
Kernel-owned deliberately: neither can honestly be extracted from the
contract document (the former is a property of which contract versions
this installed Kernel understands, not something the contract can
declare about itself; the latter is Kernel's own codified reading of
HARD_RULE 2's prose, not enumerable data).

Re-ran the existing kernel/tests/test_kernel.py suite unchanged after the
refactor — all 5 assertions still passing, confirming no regression.

IMPACT

The Kernel now genuinely depends on the Instance Contract file at
runtime, not a copy of it. A future change to the contract's required
fields or sections is picked up automatically, with no separate manual
update needed in Kernel code.

NEXT_STEPS

Per the roadmap: a minimal CLI wrapping the Kernel (pace doctor, pace
context), then pace init, then adopting TuPeríMetro as the first
governed project.

APPROVED_BY

President Cley Duarte

END
